    coverage={"geography": "US-based participants", "period": "poets from the 1340s to the present; published 14 November 2024"},
    count={"value": "1,634 participants (Study 1); 696 participants (Study 2); 16,340 discrimination judgments; 100 poems", "as_of": "14 November 2024"},
    holdings=["discrimination accuracy of 46.6%, below chance, over 16,340 judgments", "AI-generated poems more likely judged human-authored than the human-authored poems", "AI-generated poems rated more favorably in qualities such as rhythm and beauty", "poems rated more highly when participants were told the poet was human (12 of 14 qualities significant)", "the participants' low experience with poetry: 90.4% read poetry a few times per year or less"],
    stated_limits=["'Our results are limited to the most recent generation of generative language models, and to people's current beliefs and biases regarding AI-generated texts.'", "the population is non-expert: 90.4% of participants read poetry a few times per year or less", "the generated poems are 'human out of the loop': the first 5 poems generated, unselected", "the authors predicted that expertise would make no difference; experience with poetry is self-reported"],
    answers=["whether non-expert, US-based readers can tell ChatGPT 3.5 poems from poems by these ten poets (below chance, in this study)", "whether being told a poem is AI-generated lowers non-expert ratings (it did, for most qualities)", "which features those readers used to judge authorship, as the authors analyse them"],
    does_not_answer=["whether AI poetry is better than human poetry: the ratings are non-expert preferences", "how expert readers or poets judge the same poems", "how authorship of a poem is constituted or named", "anything about later models or later readers' expectations (the authors' own stated limit)"],
    ls_reading="Two results sit together here: told that a poem is AI-generated, non-expert readers rate it lower, the label effect #1722 meets as a discount on origin; and untold, the same readers take the generated poems for human ones and read the human poets' complexity as machine incoherence. The population is non-expert and the generated poems unselected; the result bears on how a stated origin shifts judgment, and its title's claim of indistinguishability holds for these readers and these poems.",
    host_relation="empirical comparator",
    record_type="paper",
    question="Can non-expert readers tell AI-generated from human-written poems, and does being told a poem's origin change how they rate it (audience evaluation, by experiment)?",
    data_access="open access article on nature.com; 'All data and code used in analysis is available at an OSF repository'; preregistration at OSF",
    license="Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License",
    lifecycle={"state": "historical", "detail": "received 13 July 2024, accepted 17 October 2024, published 14 November 2024 (version of record)"},
